diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 27d8c55..f29ea73 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -56,6 +56,51 @@ The `pass.py` script can be used as a drop-in replacement for the original `pass
 ./pass.py ls
 ```
 
+## Sync
+
+Not in the original `pass`: `pass.py sync` trades the store with other devices (another computer,
+a phone) through a *drop zone*, a directory every device can reach and nobody has to trust: a
+local path (a USB stick, a synced folder) or `sftp://user@host[:port][/dir]`.
+
+Each device publishes one file there, `<device>.bundle.gpg`: a git bundle of its whole history,
+signed with its OpenPGP key and encrypted to every device. The drop zone sees those files and
+nothing else: no entry name, no history. Devices are equals: each takes another's changes when you
+have looked at them, and merges them with its own.
+
+```bash
+# Once, on each device: its name, where the drop zone is, which key signs, who the others are
+./pass.py sync setup pc sftp://drop@example.org:2222 <own-key-fingerprint>
+./pass.py sync peer laptop <laptop-key-fingerprint>   # its public key must be in gpg, and trusted
+
+./pass.py sync push            # publish the store
+./pass.py sync fetch           # receive what the other devices published
+./pass.py sync log laptop      # what taking laptop's changes would do
+./pass.py sync diff laptop     # the same, decrypted
+./pass.py sync merge laptop    # take them, then publish
+./pass.py sync                 # where everything stands
+```
+
+A device with no store yet does `sync setup` and `sync peer` in an empty directory, then `fetch`
+and `merge`: it adopts the other's store.
+
+What `merge` does:
+
+- An entry changed on one side only is taken. An entry **moved** on one side and changed on the
+  other ends with the new content under the new name.
+- Where both sides changed the same entry differently, nothing is taken until you say which to
+  keep: `sync merge laptop --mine name` or `--theirs name`.
+- Who reads a folder (`.gpg-id`) can be changed on any device, but **a reader is always a device**:
+  a `.gpg-id` naming any other key is neither published nor taken. A device that adds itself where
+  it cannot read has only asked: `sync log` shows the entries as `stale`, and taking the change
+  re-encrypts them. Two different changes to one folder's readers: `--mine folder/` or `--theirs folder/`.
+- Only entries and `.gpg-id` files travel. A store holding anything else in git (`.extensions/`,
+  a symbolic link, a note) is neither published nor taken.
+
+`ssh` options (a key, a port in `~/.ssh/config`) apply as usual; more can be set with
+`git config pass-sync.sftp-options "-i ~/.ssh/dropzone"` in the store. The protocol, a drop zone
+server, a phone client and the scenarios all three are tested with live in
+[c4ffein-work/playground](https://github.com/c4ffein-work/playground), under `ios-apps/apps/pass`.
+
 ## Testing
 
 The Python implementation is designed to be fully compatible with the original bash script and should pass all the tests in `submodules/password-store/tests/`. Additional tests have also been written in an automated way, see [the documentation about Claude Code](chat_history/00-INDEX.md)
